A||||8||JohnAC||3.8 or bored 2.5?||||1||
Z||000000||JohnAC||07-11-2001||01:33 AM||||I was originally gonna get the modkit and put a Bonneville V6 in my Fiero.  Then one of my friends said that was a bad idea and that I should just bored out the stock 2.5 and put a twin turbo on it?!  Is there any reason to do one or the other?  What are the benefits?  Any help in this matter would be greatly appreciated.||||reg||1
Z||000001||JayUSA77||07-11-2001||02:05 AM||||Wanna take advice, take it one step further and go V-8.  The price for the 3.8 will cost about the same but lets think about which one will have more power. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>-J<P>------------------<BR>86' Black Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||Mach10||07-11-2001||03:10 AM||||Well, boring out a 2.5 will probably result in tripping the Cops radar at over 150mph. Now, if you could the rest of the Fiero, and not just parts of the engine, to do that...<P>I'd go with the 3800SC-II. I'm more of a fan of powerhouse v6s in this particular car. If for no other reason that I like 6s   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>*EDIT*<BR>However, if you want to keep the 4, that's fine. While I prefer the v6 (I always have in ANY car  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">), I also think that the Duc is under-rated. I'm fairly confident that you can get at least some decent power out of it without it self-destructing. Just make sure you slap a rev-limiter, and for God's sake, build that bottom end up TOUGH.<p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 07-11-2001).]||||reg||7
Z||000003||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||07-11-2001||04:28 AM||||Either approach is going to be lots of work and pretty expensive.  In a world full of speed limits and high tech new cars, the expense is not easy to justify.<P>Warning: boring out an Iron Duke is risky.  The blocks can and do crack.  Mine did with a 0.020" overbore.  Adding 1 turbo increases this risk dramatically.  Going with twin turbos is asking for trouble IMHO.  <P>My advice is to go with a header and cam on your duke.  Overbore the cylinders only as needed and if needed.  You will get some more power, but nothing shocking.  Maybe 140 HP?<P>For big power, then you want to build a Super Duty (big $$$) or go with a 3800 series II ($$$, hard work, TIME, lots of TIME).<P>You gotta ask yourself:<P>"Is it worth putting $5000 to $10000 in a Fiero?"  <P>We decided it was not.  Our Fiero continues to have a relatively stock Iron Duke and now a 2000 Ford Contour SVT (200HP stock) is  sitting in our driveway.  <P>Here's why:<P>The Fiero is a great car, but unless you are a super-genius car guy you can't beat 15 years of technology, mass production, and teams of engineers on your own.   <P>You will need to work on the brakes, suspension, and more to make the car safe with more than 200 HP.  Adding ABS would require a lot of engineering.  Traction control might be easier, but still lots of knowledge, know-how, and skill.  There is just too much technology in the cars of today to overcome with an engine swap alone.  You don't want to turn your Fiero into a straight line 60s muscle car do you?  <P>Set goals that are more reasonable for your car.  A Fiero is what it is.  You can make them fast and fun, but remember that you are driving on the street and like most of us, you are not Mario Andretti.  Mario expects more than just horsepower from a car anyway.  <P>Set your car up so you can handle it.  Set your car up so it can handle the power even at WOT.  A 300 HP V8 means that you cannot floor it all the time.  If you do drive a hot V8 Fiero hard, then all sorts of structural problems are possible/likely.  <P>Do you want to drive it carefully?  Hell NO!  So set a reasonable goal.  If you want to see a huge increase in performance, then go with a Series II V6.  This is all you need to go fast in a straight line.  Stopping it with stock brakes may be a real challenge, however.  <P>Another high power route to consider is a turbo 2.8/3.1/3.4 pushrod V6.  The swap may be a little easier than a 3800 (maybe not) as a lot of the factory V6 parts from GM cars can be used.<P>But really, are you sure you want to spend all that money and time on a car with a kelly blue book of $2k?  <P>Why not just do a mild Duke build or swap to the 3.4L pushrod V6?  The car will be a lot faster and yet the Fiero chassis will be sufficient with only a few suggested mods (rear sway bar, urethane bushings, Koni struts/shocks, and some other "off the shelf" mods.)  Maybe you could get the whole car done this way for less than $5K.<P>The 3800 swap is a good choice, but you are going to do lots of wiring, welding, and spend lots of money.  What if you run out of cash or patience 1/2 way through the project?  <P>This happens more often than you think to other people/dreamers.  Are you sure you want to blow $5K and three months of your time?  After that, you still have all the chassis mods to do . . .<P>Think it over.  Maybe the desire to have the fastest car in town is too expensive and unrealistic to pursue.  Save yourself the money and just accept your Fiero as it was designed to be: one of the best handling cars of it's time.<P>Then, with all the money you saved, make a down payment on a new Firebird Formula or Mustang GT.  Hell, you could go for a new BMW 325.  How about a VR6 GTI? The cars of today are very impressive indeed.  In the long run, they are probably a better investment in terms of safety, reliability, and resale value.<P>25 years from now, the big dollar Fieros will probably be stock 88 GTs and Formulas along with stock 1984 Indy pace cars.  As we go overboard modifying every Fiero we can get our hands on, we just increase the value of an all original low-mile concours Fiero.<P>So, all that money and time you spend will be lost in a very short time.  If the car is not reliable after all that work, then you might lose the dependable little car you are dreaming of hot rodding right now. <P>Again, think it over.  Moderation is the key to happiness.  Let some other guy spend his life savings on wildly escalating expectations.  ||||reg||11
Z||000004||KRMFiero||07-11-2001||06:39 AM||||Wow great post LuckyTheyWereEverMade! <BR>If I were you I would not attempt to turbocharge/bore the 2.5 block, if you want to stay with a 4 cyl. go look for a superduty it could handle the twin turbo and stuff.<BR>I would Eather get a 3800 Series II normaly asperated or if you have a few extra bucks get the supercharged - Reason is they are very raliable engines and will provide you will all the speed you'll need.<P>If your low on money but stil want to get more speed cheak out the 4.X engine conversions here: <A HREF="http://members.fortunecity.com/bubbajoexx" TARGET=_blank>http://members.fortunecity.com/bubbajoexx</A> <P>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000005||Galen||07-11-2001||06:48 AM||||Since when was the Fiero body not 'strong' enough to handle a v8? <P>I admit, a fiero is exactly that, a 80's merican econo-sports-thingie-car.. And by no means are you gonna make very much if any money off a resale if you do a 3800 s/c, 350, N*, or many other engine mods. But let me ask you this, how many of us expect to 'resale' our cars anyways? Well ya never know what will happen and maybe you will have to. Is that lost money? Lost time?  Is taking a Cruise lost money or time? <P>And I've said it before, I'll say it again and I will stand behind it, A 'Low-mile' Fiero or any car, to me, isn't worth very much. A Stock car? nah, not worth alot. I know some of ya'll will say I'm wrong on this, but a low milage car that is garaged, never driven, just looked at & cleaned is a waste of a car that was meant to be driven, Meant to be made into someones OWN car. Not a model of what the car was when it was made. If you want one of those, go to Wal-Mart and buy a model car. <P>a TT Duke, dont try it. Go with a 3800... thats my opinion.<BR><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/redcreature.gif">   <BR><FONT SIZE="1">To whom it may concern, The preceeding message was the opinion of this person and not of this forum. If you do not like it. Oh well</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cozmo||07-11-2001||08:48 AM||||Awesome post Lucky...<BR>A car is a SYSTEM and all aspects need to be taken into account when doing "serious surgery".<BR>The 3800 is one of the best engines ever made,  I'm going to swap one into my '87 as soon as I clear out some shop space to do the job.<BR><B>C02M0</B>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Hartz||07-11-2001||09:04 AM||||Lucky - good words of warning, but a couple of alternative thoughts...<P>Not everyone is worried about resale value.  I personally doubt that ANY Fiero, be it a low mileage 88GT with t-tops, a mint Indy, or a SBC V8 conversion, will ever make anyone rich.  If anyone DOES get rich playing with Fieros - please share the secret - I want to know!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>Everyone always mentions brakes.  Yeah, the Fiero brakes like a 17 year old car with marginal brakes, but if you start with the stock brake's performance as a base, why does an engine swap necessitate a brake upgrade?  I still drive my Fieros within the speedlimit - usually  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> - so I should be able to stop from 60 MPH the same as any other Fiero, no?<P>To get back on topic - either any of the options mentioned to date will be hard.  If you're smart enough to do one, you're probably smart enough to do any of them, so go with whatever appeals to you - but know what you're getting into before you take the plunge.<P>Best of luck,<BR>Hartz<P>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||stimpy||07-11-2001||10:06 AM||||Your friend is an idiot. Get new friends. ||||reg||1
